This uses Brent's cycle detection method. One pointer moves forward, while the other marks the end of a growing jump block. After finding a meeting point, the code walks once around the cycle to count its nodes.

It does not change any links in the list, so the input list stays unchanged.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findLengthOfLoop(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head.next;
        int power = 1;
        int blockLength = 1;

        while (slow != fast) {
            if (power == blockLength) {
                slow = fast;
                power *= 2;
                blockLength = 0;
            }

            fast = fast.next;
            blockLength++;
        }

        int length = 1;
        fast = fast.next;
        while (fast != slow) {
            fast = fast.next;
            length++;
        }

        return length;
    }
}
```

A self-loop reaches the meeting point immediately and returns 1. A normal list eventually stops at `null` and returns 0.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This keeps every node in a `HashSet` while walking forward. The first node that appears again is the start of the repeated part, so its stored position gives the loop length.

Dry run: for `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 2`, the positions are 0, 1, 2, 3. Node 2 appears again at position 4, so the length is `4 - 1 = 3`.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int findLengthOfLoop(ListNode head) {
        Map<ListNode, Integer> firstPosition = new HashMap<>();
        ListNode current = head;
        int position = 0;

        while (current != null) {
            if (firstPosition.containsKey(current)) {
                return position - firstPosition.get(current);
            }

            firstPosition.put(current, position);
            current = current.next;
            position++;
        }

        return 0;
    }
}
```

The extra map makes this easier to follow, but it uses more memory than the two-pointer method.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stored node positions.

A slow pointer moves one node at a time and a fast pointer moves two nodes at a time. If they meet, a loop exists. From that meeting node, walk around once to count the loop length.

For an empty list or a list without a cycle, the method returns 0.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findLengthOfLoop(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode *slow = head;
        ListNode *fast = head;

        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;

            if (slow == fast) {
                int length = 1;
                ListNode *current = slow->next;

                while (current != slow) {
                    current = current->next;
                    length++;
                }

                return length;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the pointers make only a few passes through the list.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

